\subsection*{Part A: Indicate your hypothesis}
\begin{steps}	
       \newstep[] Obtain the unknown compound, the blanks, and the reagents.
        \newstep[] Compare the unknown with the blanks and guess which blank looks like the unknown. That is your hypothesis. Write down the name of your guess on the results page.
        \newstep[] Wash a 100 mL graduated cylinder and 400 mL beaker. Place a pea-size portion of the unknown in the beaker, and add 150 mL of water using the cylinder to measure it. Swirl until all the solid has been totally or partially dissolved.
     \newstep[] Set aside the remaining portion of your unknown.
    

  \import{\chapterpath/}{goodlabpractice}

\end{steps}

\subsection*{Part B: Testing for gas}
\begin{steps}	
 \newstep[] In this first experiment you will use the solid version of the blanks, not the droppers. Obtain six test tubes. In each test tube, you will add a blank reagent in a solid state, that is, one blank per test tube.
 To each test tube, you will add a pea-size sample of a different blank in a solid state using its spatula. You will add the unknown in the sixth test tube. 
 This way, place a pea-size solid sample of NaCl in the first test tube. Add a pea-size sample of \ce{NaI} in the second test tube, and so on with the rest of the blanks, \ce{Na2CO3}, \ce{Na2HPO4}, \ce{Na2SO4} and your solid unknown in the following test tubes. Mark your test tubes for recognition.
    \newstep[] Add five drops of \ce{HNO3} {\color{red}\danger} to each test tube and record your observations in the results table: \textit{GE, for gas evolution, NGE for no gas evolution}.
    \newstep[] Discharge the solutions from the test tubes into the right waste container. Wash the test tubes, and rinse them with distilled water.
 
\end{steps}

\subsection*{Part C: Testing for a barium precipitate}
\begin{steps}	

    \newstep[] For the following test will use only liquid samples in the dropper, not the powders. Use the solutions of NaCl, \ce{NaI}, \ce{Na2CO3}, \ce{Na2HPO4} and \ce{Na2SO4} found in the laboratory, and the solution of your unknown prepared in Step 3 for the remaining tests in this practice.
    \newstep[] Using six clean test tubes, add 20 drops of each of the solutions as well as the unknown to separate test tube. In short, five of the test tubes should have a different blank and the sixth test tube should have the unknown solution. Add 3 drops of a solution of ammonia (\ce{NH3}) in each test tube {\color{red}\danger} and swirl until solved.
    \newstep[] Add five drops of the \ce{Ba(NO3)2} {\color{red}\danger} solution to each of the test tubes. Shake gently to obtain homogeneity. Examine each test tube carefully and look at the results. Record your observations: \textit{P for precipitate, NP for no precipitate}.
    \end{steps}

