\subsection*{  Part D: Testing for silver precipitate}
     \begin{steps}
    \newstep[] Using six clean test tubes, add 20 drops of each of the solutions including the unknown to separate test tubes (e.g. NaCl, \ce{NaI}, \ce{Na2CO3}, \ce{Na2HPO4}, \ce{Na2SO4}, and unknown). In short, five of the test tubes should have a different blank and the sixth test tube should have the unknown solution.
       \newstep[] Add five drops of the nitric acid \ce{AgNO3} solution to each test tube. Record your observations: \textit{P for precipitate, NP for no precipitate}. Include the color of the precipitate.
\end{steps}
     \subsection*{  Part E: Testing with thymol blue}
     \begin{steps}
    \newstep[] Using six clean test tubes, add 20 drops of each of the solutions including the unknown to separate test tubes (e.g. NaCl, \ce{NaI}, \ce{Na2CO3}, \ce{Na2HPO4}, \ce{Na2SO4}, and unknown). In short, five of the test tubes should have a different blank and the sixth test tube should have the unknown solution.
      \newstep[] Add five drops of the thymol blue solution to each test tube. Shake each test tube gently before recording your observations: \textit{write down Blue, Yellow green, or Red}.
\end{steps}
     \subsection*{ Part F: Identifying the unknown compound}
     \begin{steps}
    \newstep[] The unknown is one of these compounds:  \ce{NaCl}, \ce{NaI}, \ce{Na2CO3}, \ce{Na2HPO4}, \ce{Na2SO4}. You should be able to identify your unknown compound by matching its reactions with those of one of the known samples.
    \newstep[]  When done with the experiment, clean up all test tubes using a test tube brush and soapy water.
\end{steps}
